            "body": "> > This also results in two progress reports\r\n> \r\n> Not ideal imo (but also not worth any \"hacks\").\r\n\r\nThe extra reasoning for why I think this is acceptable is that two affected cases are (in my opinion) not that common compared to the main `:packupdate` with confirmation buffer one.\r\n\r\nFor posterity, the hacks I thought about:\r\n- For force update -jJust don't show the second progress report (for checking out, since it should be rather quick).\r\n- For update code action - maybe pass the private flag to `vim.pack.update()` that disables the second progress report.\r\n\r\nBut at least with force update I think two progress report is clearer, since it is regular update (\"downloading/competing\" progress report) plus the \"force\" part (applying updates).",
